{{right|''Emerson'', Clubs.}}}}
{{smaller block|When joined to a personal or relative pronoun in the plural, ''all'' may precede, but now usually follows, the pronoun.}}
{{smaller block|''All we'' like sheep have gone astray. {{right|Isa. liii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|And ''we all'' do fade as a leaf. {{right|Isa. lxiv. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|Be ''ye all'' of one mind. {{right|1 Pet. iii. 8.}}}}
{{smaller block|That ''they all'' may be one. {{right|John xvii. 21.}}}}
{{smaller block|The alternative construction is ''all of us, all of them'', etc. (see II., 2); or the two constructions may stand together.}}
{{smaller block|''We all of us'' complain of the shortness of time.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 93.}}}}
{{smaller block|The adjective ''all'', with a singular or plural noun, is often separated from its subject, especially by the verb ''be'' (expressed, or in the present participle often omitted), and, being thus apparently a part of the predicate, assumes a transitional position, and may equally well be regarded as an adverb, meaning altogether, wholly: as, the house was ''all'' dark; he was ''all'' ears; the poor horse was ''all'' skin and bones; the papers were ''all'' in confusion; it was ''all'' a mistake; it is ''all'' gone.}}
{{smaller block|He is ''all'' for fasting. {{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 245.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>She follow’d my poor father’s body,
Like Niobe, ''all'' tears.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|He has also rebuilt y<sup>e</sup> parsonage house, ''all'' of stone, very neate and ample. {{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Sept. 9, 1677.}}}}
{{smaller block|'''All Fools’ day.''' See ''fool''<sup>1</sup>.—'''All hands''', the whole company; ''naut.'', the whole crew.—'''All my eye.''' See ''eye''<sup>1</sup>.—'''All Saints’ day.''' See ''saint''.—'''All Souls’ day.''' See ''soul''.—'''For all the world.''' See ''world''.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' as ''pron.'' [Absolute use of the adj.] '''1.''' The whole quantity or amount; the whole; the aggregate; the total: in a singular sense.
{{smaller block|And Laban . . . said, . . . ''All'' that thou seest is mine.
{{right|Gen. xxxi. 43.}}}}
